---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Calculator from '../components/Calculator.astro';
import {byId} from '../data/routes';
import {calculatorTasks} from '../data/calculator-tasks';
import {plannerSampleReference as liveExample} from '../data/seo-reference';
import GeoSourceList from '../components/GeoSourceList.astro';
---
<Base home title="Quilt Cut Planner — Fabric Yardage & Cutting Plans" description="Plan quilt fabric and cutting layouts with free backing, binding, yardage and border calculators. Get feasible cut plans and printable shopping lists.">
<div class="page-shell">
<section class="page-intro page-intro--home" data-ui-hero>
 <div class="page-heading">
  <span class="eyebrow-with-line">— THE MAKER'S TOOLKIT</span>
  <h1>Plan your quilt fabric.<br/><span class="hero-emphasis">Cut with confidence.</span></h1>
  <p class="answer-first-lead" data-answer-first="home"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> There is no single yardage number for every quilt. Calculate backing, batting, binding, borders and each quilt-top fabric separately, using finished outside dimensions, actual usable fabric width and the construction method for that material.</p>
  <p>Free quilt fabric calculators for backing, binding, borders and yardage, with real rectangular cut layouts, seam allowances and printable shopping lists. Less guessing, more making.</p>
  <div class="feature-pills"><span>✓ Physical cutting plans</span><span>✓ No account</span><span>✓ Local project files</span></div>
  <div class="hero-actions"><a class="hero-main-cta" href="#calculator-workspace">Start with your quilt <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="hero-sub-cta" href="/calculators/">Explore the full planner</a></div>
  <p class="hero-reassurance">Free tools · No sign-up · Your measurements stay on this device</p>
 </div>
 <div class="mini-feature mini-feature--crafted"><div class="mini-top"><span>MEASURE TWICE, CUT ONCE</span><span>QUILT CUT PLANNER / 2026</span></div><img src="/images/hub.webp" width="640" height="420" alt="Original illustration of patterned fabric squares arranged in a quilting cutting workspace" fetchpriority="high" loading="eager"/>
  <svg class="hero-thread" viewBox="0 0 580 280" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M-12 185 Q 75 95, 154 156 T 321 144 T 598 70" pathLength="100" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="1.2 1.2" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
  <span class="hero-fabric-seal" aria-hidden="true">MADE<br/>TO<br/>MEASURE</span><div class="mini-bottom"><b>Make room for the joy.</b><span>✳ Made to measure</span></div></div>
</section>
<GeoSourceList tool="hub"/>
<section class="content-block home-workspace-section" style="margin-top:22px">
 <div class="editorial-head"><span class="eyebrow-with-line">— START RIGHT HERE</span><h2>How does the complete quilt cutting planner work?</h2><p>Try the live {liveExample.centerWidthIn} × {liveExample.centerHeightIn} inch CENTER-size example with a {liveExample.borderPerSideIn}-inch finished border on each edge, producing a {liveExample.finalWidthIn} × {liveExample.finalHeightIn} inch outside quilt. The calculator verifies rectangular cutting geometry against entered fabric widths; real-paper scale and physical fabric cuts still require manual checks.</p></div>
 <Calculator tool="hub" sample={byId('hub').sample}/>
</section>
<section class="content-block" data-geo-home-guide aria-label="How quilt fabric calculations work">
 <div class="editorial-head"><span class="eyebrow-with-line">— QUICK GUIDANCE</span><h2>How much fabric does a quilt need?</h2>
 <p><strong>There is no universal yardage for a quilt size.</strong> The answer depends on your finished quilt dimensions, pattern pieces, borders, seam allowances, fabric widths and permitted grain directions. Backing, binding and each quilt-top fabric must be calculated separately.</p></div>
 <ol>
  <li><strong>Measure the outside quilt.</strong> Include all borders or clearly identify a center-only measurement.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose the right tool and material.</strong> Record the actual usable roll width and physical cut-piece dimensions.</li>
  <li><strong>Check each layout before buying.</strong> Confirm row and panel fit, seam take-up, shrinkage and shop rounding.</li>
 </ol>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Quilt fabric calculator selection table">
  <table class="breakdown-table"><caption>Choose a calculator by the material and question</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Material or task</th><th scope="col">What determines the result</th><th scope="col">Calculator</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Quilt backing</th><td>Finished size, overage, panels and usable WOF</td><td><a href="/quilt-backing-calculator/">Backing calculator</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Straight-grain binding</th><td>Perimeter, cut strip width and join loss</td><td><a href="/quilt-binding-calculator/">Binding calculator</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Butted borders</th><td>Layer-by-layer cut width and side/top lengths</td><td><a href="/quilt-border-calculator/">Border calculator</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">One-piece batting</th><td>Overage and available roll width</td><td><a href="/quilt-batting-calculator/">Batting calculator</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Quilt-top colours</th><td>Separate per-SKU cutting layouts</td><td><a href="/quilt-fabric-calculator/">Quilt fabric calculator</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Repeated fabric pieces</th><td>Cut-piece size, count, grain and WOF</td><td><a href="/fabric-yardage-calculator/">Fabric yardage calculator</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Sashing and cornerstones</th><td>Block grid, internal strip count and seam allowance</td><td><a href="/quilt-sashing-calculator/">Sashing calculator</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Yards to inches</th><td>Exact linear conversion (1 yard = 36 inches)</td><td><a href="/fabric-yards-to-inches/">Length converter</a></td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
 </div>
 <p>See <a href="/about/#sources">reference sources and calculation limits</a>. These examples are not physical sewing certification.</p>
</section>
<section class="content-block tool-choice" data-tool-choice>
 <div class="editorial-head"><span class="eyebrow-with-line">— FIND YOUR CALCULATOR</span><h2>What would you like to calculate?</h2><p>Choose by the actual fabric-cutting problem. Every question leads to one calculator, not a duplicate keyword page.</p></div>
 <nav class="task-grid" aria-label="Find a quilt calculator by your cutting task">
 {calculatorTasks.map(task=>{
   const route=byId(task.id);
   return <a class="task-card" href={'/'+route.slug+'/'} data-task={task.id}>
      <span class="task-card-type">{task.label}</span>
      <strong>{task.question}</strong>
      <span class="task-card-detail">{task.detail}</span>
      <span class="task-card-link">Use the {route.nav.toLowerCase()} tool <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span>
    </a>;
 })}
 </nav>
</section>
<section class="sample-block"><div><span class="eyebrow-with-line">— WHY WE MADE THIS</span><h2>Why does fabric geometry matter more than area alone?</h2><p>A square-inch area estimate can hide impossible cuts. Quilt Cut Planner checks usable width, grain direction and actual rectangular placement before giving you a purchase quantity. Where sewing geometry is unverified, the tool tells you clearly instead of guessing.</p><a href="/calculators/">View all planning tools ↗</a></div><span class="sample-icon" aria-hidden="true">✳</span></section>
</div>
</Base>
